      "raw": "After analyzing all three views, we observe that each view contains three garments: a black long-sleeved top (seen from the back), a black skirt with lace trim, and a white button-up shirt (seen from the front). The garments are shown in different arrangements across the views, but their visual characteristics \u2014 fabric, color, construction, and context \u2014 remain consistent.\n\nWe are to assign each region ID to a garment track, ensuring that each track includes at least one region from every view. Since there are three views, each track must include at least one region from each of the three views.\n\nLet\u2019s map the regions:\n\n- **R001 (View 00_all_present_v2)**: black top, back view \u2192 matches R004 (View 00_all_present_v1) and R007 (View 00_all_present_v0)\n- **R002 (View 00_all_present_v2)**: black skirt with lace \u2192 matches R005 (View 00_all_present_v1) and R008 (View 00_all_present_v0)\n- **R003 (View 00_all_present_v2)**: white shirt, front view \u2192 matches R006 (View 00_all_present_v1) and R009 (View 00_all_present_v0)\n\nEach of these three garments appears in every view, and their corresponding regions are consistent in appearance and context.\n\nThus, we can form three tracks:\n\n1. Black long-sleeved top (back view) \u2014 regions R001, R004, R007\n2. Black skirt with lace trim \u2014 regions R002, R005, R008\n3. White button-up shirt (front view) \u2014 regions R003, R006, R009\n\nNo other regions are present, and no region is shared between different garments.\n\n---\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tracks\": [\n    {\n      \"garment\": \"black long-sleeved top, back view\",\n      \"regions\": [\"R001\", \"R004\", \"R007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"garment\": \"black skirt with lace trim\",\n      \"regions\": [\"R002\", \"R005\", \"R008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"garment\": \"white button-up shirt, front view\",\n      \"regions\": [\"R003\", \"R006\", \"R009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
